// Darwin entropy source for the Stage-2 port of runtime/rand/ (#1368).
// See ../rand.bit for why the port is split by directory rather than switching
// on the OS inside one file.
//
// ENTROPY POLICY IS DELIBERATELY STRICT, and is inherited verbatim from the
// Fill ONLY from the OS CSPRNG. Never a userspace PRNG, never a weak or
// zero fallback. On failure report it and let the one caller turn that into a
// fatal panic — silently handing back predictable "random" bytes is a security
// defect far worse than a crash.

// `arc4random_buf` is always present on Darwin and is a CSPRNG there (ChaCha20,
// seeded from the kernel). It cannot fail, which is why it has no return value
// to check, which is why there is no error to propagate.
//
// Reached through `extern function` (§11.7) rather than a raw syscall because
// Apple publishes no stable syscall numbers and reserves the right to renumber
// them; libSystem is Darwin's only supported kernel entry point. ABI.md §21
// names this exact function as the macOS source.
extern function arc4random_buf(buf: *u8, n: i64)

// Fills the `n` packed bytes at `p` from the OS CSPRNG. Returns true on
// success.
//
// The natural signature is `fill(buf) !void`, where the sole error is
// `error.Entropy`. Bit's `!` error type is GC-managed, which the unmanaged
// subset cannot use, so the port reports failure as a plain `bool` — the
// honest unmanaged equivalent of a single-error union. The caller
// (`bit_rt_random_bytes`, #1367) turns false into the same fatal panic that
// `catch fatal("random_bytes: OS CSPRNG failed")` produces today.
//
// On this platform the result is unconditionally true, because
// `arc4random_buf` cannot fail. Keeping the `bool` anyway means both platform
// modules present one signature, so the caller has no per-OS branch.
//
// `@nosplit` (#1667). `p` is an interior pointer into the fresh `string`
// `rtRandomBytes` just allocated and holds only as a raw `int` — no stack map
// names it (ABI.md §5 walks the running task's stack PRECISELY), so a
// back-edge poll anywhere in this subtree lets the collector sweep the buffer
// being filled (#1656). §10.3.1 admits `extern function` (§11.7), which is the
// only non-trivial form here.
export @nosplit function entropyFill(p: *u8, n: int): bool {
  if (n <= 0) { // a zero-length fill is a no-op, not an error
    return true
  }
  arc4random_buf(p, i64(n))
  return true
}
